<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 2021 Situations and Equations"><h2>Warm-up — Notice and Wonder</h2><p>A bar has total128, three equal segments of8, and unknown b. Thus b=128−24=<strong>104</strong>. Naming it with a letter does not change the amount.</p><h2>Activity 1 — Mai's Beads</h2><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source situation</th><th>Equation</th><th>Meaning of unknown</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>104+2×n=124</td><td>One pack</td><td>n=10</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>n+10+10=124</td><td>Starting beads</td><td>n=104</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>104+2×10=n</td><td>Final beads</td><td>n=124</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Draw an independently authored three-part bar for each structure. The source publisher's three bar images have only alt-text/URL evidence and are not copied.</p><h2>Activity 2 — Kiran's Mancala</h2><p>104 stones, 6 pits with3 stones each. Equation r=104−(6×3)=<strong>86</strong>. Explain why the answer is fewer than104, and check that86+18=104. The original Mancala photograph and gated How Many Beads? Cool-down are not reproduced.</p><p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-3/lesson-19/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher lesson</a></p></section>